  "query_preview": "..\nA resurgence of another monsoon surge, induced by various fast-\ndeveloping systems embedded within the vast monsoon trough to the\nnorth, is expected to bring increasing winds, showers and \nthunderstorms to the region. At this time, the surge south of a \nregenerated Tropical Depression Kujira (14W) is expected to trip over\nTinian and Saipan, with 20-25 mph southwesterlies occasionally \ngusting to 35 mph near potentially heavy showers Monday. The leading \nedge of these surges are expected to anchor themselves over Tinian \nand Saipan through midweek, as a secondary disturbance follows an \neastward- propagating Kujira and induces a much more persistent surge\nthereafter. This will allow convection to then shift southward, \nspreading on-and-off showers and thunderstorms across the rest of the\nCNMI and Guam. However, these conditions are not expected to be \nconducive for flash flooding, so a Flood Watch is not yet warranted \nbut may be considered for Tinian and Saipan the next day or so. \nConditions are expected to improve considerably in the second half of\nthe week once these systems move away from the area and a tropical \nridge forms in western Micronesia, introducing drier diffusive flow \nacross the Marianas for the rest of the week.",
